"""One-off (P4b): appends the game-layer decisions D-070 ... D-078 to docs/decisions.md (LF, no blank lines)."""
from pathlib import Path

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
ROWS = [
    "| D-070 | 2026-10-14 | Record / replay (brief 4.12, test 15): F5 captures the complete simulation state (`WorldState`: every native array of the world found by reflection, plus the bodies' spawn poses) and then records each frame's substep count and input (`InputRecording`, gzip `.hrec` with the snapshot, inputs also as CSV, a hash of geometry.json + params.json); F6 restores the snapshot and plays the frames with the recorded partition (bit-identical) or, in slow motion and while stepping, looks the input up per substep. Scripted scenarios may start from a respawn (pose and speed) instead of a snapshot. `Respawn` also restarts the collision grid's rebuild schedule (`clock[1]`), so what follows a respawn does not depend on the substeps before it. | A snapshot makes a recording startable at any moment, damage included, and the reflection keeps it complete when state arrays are added; one input per frame is what the game's sources produce. Test 15: replay from the snapshot bit-identical, the 250 fps partition bit-identical too (the near lists and the grid margin make the partition irrelevant), the file round trip identical, respawn starts after a different history identical; the snapshot is 3.8 MB raw, a minute of driving with it 0.8 MB on disk. In the player the 60 s drive recording replays bit-identically (G-Drive). |",
    "| D-071 | 2026-10-14 | Cameras (`CameraRig`): the chase camera damps its offset from the centre of mass, not its position (constant distance at any speed; heading lag 0.3 s, soft height); the bumper camera sits 9 cm ahead of the front-most fascia node at 0.62 m, riding on the bumper-beam mount; the cockpit camera rides on the cabin mount (sill, roof rails, tunnel) with the best-fit rotation of D-062 and an 85 deg horizontal field of view (cockpit.json); a free camera (mouse, I J K L U O, gamepad). Chase distance and fields of view come from settings.json. | The first chase camera damped its position and fell 3-4 m behind at 75 km/h; the skeleton's bumper mount (0.25 m above the bumper beam) put the lens inside the fascia plastic. The skeleton is not re-exported for this (it would change geometry.json and the P4 crash evidence); the offset is computed in the rig. |",
    "| D-072 | 2026-10-14 | Live cockpit (D-021 part 2, `CockpitInstruments`): the tach and speedometer needles, steering wheel and pedals are taken off the skin and drawn as rigid copies of their meshes that follow the best-fit frame of the nodes they were skinned to and turn about their pivots from cockpit.json (Blender axes to Unity: a rotation sense flips with the axes); steering wheel = steer x 1.3 turns. The three mirrors get render textures from cameras at the glass (interior: the eye ray reflected about cockpit.json's normal; side mirrors: 6 deg outboard of straight back, as a driver sets them, since the exported glass is flat and faces straight back), mapped across the glass with the image flipped left to right, rendered only in the cockpit view. | A needle cannot turn while it is skinned to the dash nodes; the rigid copy rides with the dash in a crash and keeps the Blender kinematics checked in P1b. Projective mapping in the mirror's own frame works with any UV layout of the glass. |",
    "| D-073 | 2026-10-14 | Debug views (brief 16): N node-beam view alone (paint hidden), Shift+N the same as an x-ray overlay over the paint; H beam stress heat-map (opens the node-beam view), Shift+H deformation heat-map on the visual mesh. Nodes are drawn as small crosses, orange while in contact. The deformation map colours each vertex by its distance from the model-pose position (UV3, written by the skin postprocessor, `CarSkinPostprocessor` version 2) carried by the cabin floor's rigid frame (`CarProbe.Pose`); wheels and suspension keep their paint. | The brief binds N and H; the second map and the overlay are on the same keys with Shift. Displacement from the cabin-floor frame is zero for an undeformed car at any pose (uniform blue on the drive loop), so colour means deformation; spinning and steered wheels would read as damage. |",
    "| D-074 | 2026-10-14 | Reset and repair: R puts a fresh car (damage cleared, settled, at rest) at the last safe pose, recorded every 0.25 s of simulated time while the car is upright (cabin up.y > 0.95), has broken no beam for 1 s and keeps 0.6 m from every obstacle (the ground plane excepted); Shift+R puts a fresh car upright where this one is, moved back (up to 5 m, then forward) until the undeformed car clears every obstacle by 5 cm; teleports (pause menu) settle the car at a spawn point and launch it at the chosen speed. Any of them ends a recording (saved) or a replay. | A pose taken while the car rests against a wall after a crash would respawn the undamaged (longer) car inside the wall; the clearance and damage conditions exclude it. |",
    "| D-075 | 2026-10-14 | Input path and latency: `LiveDriverInput` runs before `SimRunner` (execution order -200 vs -100), so a key read in a frame drives that frame's substeps; ABS and traction control default on for the live driver (F7 / F8, settings.json). G-Drive measures the latency end to end in the player: a key-down event queued on a virtual keyboard through the Input System until the first rendered frame (end of frame, after the GPU skinning) whose front wheel has turned 0.25 deg; the display adds its refresh interval. | The previous order applied a key one frame late. The measurement covers the input system, the solver, the steering actuation through the knuckle nodes and the render (P4b report: about 18 ms mean, 2-3 frames at the 180 Hz display). |",
    "| D-076 | 2026-10-14 | HUD text is built at 10 Hz and drawn from cached strings (IMGUI's several OnGUI passes per frame had each rebuilt every line and called the world metrics); the energy bars (kinetic, elastic + torsion + pressure, plastic work, breaking + damping, log scale) refresh at 5 Hz; the drive scene runs with the energy ledger on. | With per-pass text the 30 s drive ran at 62 fps mean with 28 ms p99 frames; cached, 116 fps mean and 17 ms p99 on the same machine. |",
    "| D-077 | 2026-10-14 | G-Drive test (`DriveTest`, `-scenario drive` in the player, vsync on): ten latency probes at rest, then 300 s of simulated driving by the validation driver robot on a 977 m stadium loop (300 m straights at 160 km/h, R 60 m curves at 75 km/h, braking at 5.5 m/s2, ABS and TC on) in five 60 s camera / view segments (chase, cockpit with mirrors, bumper, node-beam overlay with the stress map, deformation map), the first 60 s recorded and replayed at the end. Criteria in `tools/gate_p4b.py`: real-time factor >= 0.98, 60 fps mean in every view, latency <= 50 ms, no divergence (no non-finite value, up.y >= 0.9, cabin floor residual <= 5 mm, no broken beam, node speed <= 3.5 x the car's), no structural creep, the replay bit-identical. | The brief asks for measured numbers and a 5-minute drive without divergence; a scripted drive makes them repeatable, the views spread the rendering cost, and the replay check proves the F5 / F6 path in the shipped player. |",
    "| D-078 | 2026-10-14 | Settings and tuning: settings.json next to the executable (cameras, aids, slow-motion factor, substep cap, vsync, HUD), written with the defaults on the first start; F9 reloads geometry.json and params.json from StreamingAssets and rebuilds the world without restarting (the node and beam layout must stay the same; the car respawns fresh where it was). Recordings and screenshots go to recordings/ next to the executable. | Brief 4.12 asks for a settings file and names params hot-reload a bonus; rebuilding the world is simpler and safer than patching live arrays, and the views keep only node indices. |",
]

p = ROOT / "docs/decisions.md"
s = p.read_text(encoding="utf-8").replace("\r\n", "\n").rstrip("\n")
have = [r.split(" | ")[0] for r in ROWS if r.split(" | ")[0] in s]
if have:
    raise SystemExit(f"already present: {have}")
p.write_bytes((s + "\n" + "\n".join(ROWS) + "\n").encode("utf-8"))
print(f"appended {len(ROWS)} decisions")
